{"cuisine/nigerian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/nigerian-cuisine","n":"Nigerian cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Nigeria","intro":[["Nigerian cuisine consists of dishes or food items from the hundreds of Native African ethnic groups that comprise Nigeria. Like other West African cuisines, it uses spices and herbs with palm oil or groundnut oil to create deeply flavored sauces and soups."],["Nigerian feasts can be colourful and lavish, while aromatic market and roadside snacks cooked on barbecues or fried in oil are in abundance and varied."],["Tropical fruits such as watermelon, pineapple, coconut, banana, orange, papaya and mango are mostly consumed in Nigeria."],["Nigerian cuisine, like many West African cuisines, is known for being savoury and spicy."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"nigerian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nigerian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Kitchen Butterfly","ref":"site/kitchenbutterfly-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"kitchenbutterfly.com","note":"Long-running blog by Nigerian food writer and historian Ozoz Sokoh, with Nigerian and New Nigerian recipes.","url":"https://www.kitchenbutterfly.com/about-2","suggested":true},{"name":"Sisi Yemmie","ref":"site/sisiyemmie-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"sisiyemmie.com","note":"Nigerian home cooking recipes and meal plans, paired with the SisiYemmieTV YouTube channel.","url":"https://sisiyemmie.com/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"My Everyday Lagos: Nigerian Cooking at Home and in the Diaspora","ref":"cookbook/my-everyday-lagos-nigerian-cooking-at-home-and-in-the-diaspora","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Yewande Komolafe, 2023","note":"Nigerian home cooking adapted for kitchens abroad by a New York Times Cooking contributor.","url":"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/646506/my-everyday-lagos-by-yewande-komolafe/","suggested":true},{"name":"Chop Chop: Cooking the Food of Nigeria","ref":"cookbook/chop-chop-cooking-the-food-of-nigeria","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Ozoz Sokoh, 2025","note":"Survey of Nigerian dishes and ingredients from the writer behind Kitchen Butterfly.","url":"https://www.hachette.co.uk/titles/ozoz-sokoh/chop-chop/9781648291890/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":9,"links":[["Akadi","dish/akadi"],["Edikang ikong","dish/edikang-ikong"],["Edo black soup","dish/edo-black-soup"],["Miyar zogale","dish/miyar-zogale"],["Moin moin","dish/moin-moin"],["Ofada rice","dish/ofada-rice"],["Palm nut soup","dish/palm-nut-soup"],["Sinasir","dish/sinasir"],["Suya","dish/suya"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Palm oil","ingredient/palm-oil"],["Crayfish","ingredient/crayfish"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Catharanthus roseus","ingredient/catharanthus-roseus"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Pumpkin","ingredient/pumpkin"],["Waterleaf","ingredient/waterleaf"],["Palm fruit juice","ingredient/palm-fruit-juice"],["Peanut","ingredient/peanut"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Black-eyed bean","ingredient/black-eyed-bean"],["Black-eyed pea","ingredient/black-eyed-pea"],["Honey bean","ingredient/honey-bean"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Iru","ingredient/iru"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Cayenne pepper","ingredient/cayenne-pepper"],["Goat","ingredient/goat"],["Ram","ingredient/ram"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"African cuisine"},"cuisine/paraguayan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/paraguayan-cuisine","n":"Paraguayan cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Paraguay","intro":[["Paraguayan cuisine is the set of dishes and culinary techniques of Paraguay. It has a marked influence of the Guaraní people combined with the Spanish cuisine and other marked influences coming from the immigration received by bordering countries such as Italian cuisine and German cuisine. The city of Asunción is the epicenter of the distinctive gastronomy that extends in current Paraguay and its areas of influence, which is the reason why it is considered the \"mother of the gastronomy\" of the Río de la Plata. It is worth clarifying that in the Paraguayan society, the exchange of knowledge between mestizos, creoles and cario-guaraní people occurred before the Jesuit missions."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"paraguayan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paraguayan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Camila Made: Top 15 Paraguayan Recipes","ref":"site/camilamade-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"camilamade.com","note":"Paraguayan recipe roundup on a Latin American home cooking blog.","url":"https://camilamade.com/top-15-paraguayan-recipes/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":5,"links":[["Chipá","dish/chipa"],["Dulce de batata","dish/dulce-de-batata"],["Paraguay cheese","dish/paraguay-cheese"],["Piracaldo","dish/piracaldo"],["Sopa paraguaya","dish/sopa-paraguaya"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Cassava","ingredient/cassava"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Anise","ingredient/anise"],["Sweet potato","ingredient/sweet-potato"],["Bell pepper","ingredient/bell-pepper"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Cornmeal","ingredient/cornmeal"],["Maize flour","ingredient/maize-flour"],["Paraguay cheese","ingredient/paraguay-cheese"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Within Paraguayan cooking","count":1,"links":[["Cuisine of Asunción","cuisine/cuisine-of-asuncion"]]},{"h":"More from Paraguay","count":1,"links":[["Guaraní foodways","cuisine/guarani-foodways"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/yao-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/yao-cuisine","n":"Yao cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[],"src":null,"wiki":"Yao cuisine","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"yao-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yao_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"ingredient/toast":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/toast","n":"Toast","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Toast is sliced bread that has been browned by radiant heat. The browning is the result of a Maillard reaction altering the flavor of the bread and making it crispier in texture. The firm surface is easier to spread toppings on and the warmth can help spreads such as butter reach their melting point. Toasting is a common method of making stale bread more palatable. Bread is commonly toasted using devices specifically designed for such, e.g., a toaster or a toaster oven. Toast may contain more acrylamide, caused by the browning process, which is suspected to be a carcinogen. However, claims that acrylamide in burnt food causes cancer have not been proven."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-toast"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toast_(food)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Club sandwich","dish/club-sandwich"]]],["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Melba toast","dish/melba-toast"]]],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Canapé","dish/canape"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[[